/-! # Closed factorization locus for a finite locally free source

Source: Conrad, Reductive Group Schemes, Lemma 2.1.3 and its proof, pp.58--59,
and proof of Proposition 2.1.2, p.60; Stacks Project, Tag 0B97, coefficient construction,
and Tag 01TV, finite presentation of closed immersions
(blueprint: lem-sfb-finite-locally-free-source-factorization-locus).

Every map `t : T ⟶ P` equips `T` with its induced structure map `t ≫ p` to `S`.
Thus the tests below range over all S-schemes mapping to P over S. The same applies
on every parameter base change. Closed immersions are affine, hence quasi-compact
and quasi-separated; their local finite presentation below is therefore finite presentation.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A family from a finite locally free source factors through a finitely presented closed
subscheme precisely on a finitely presented closed parameter subscheme. Both lifts are
unique, and the given pullback immersion represents the induced condition after every
parameter base change. No smoothness, connectedness or Noetherian assumption is made.

Source: Conrad, Reductive Group Schemes, Lemma 2.1.3, pp.58--59, and proof of
Proposition 2.1.2, p.60; Stacks Project, Tags 0B97 and 01TV
(blueprint: lem-sfb-finite-locally-free-source-factorization-locus).

Proof plan:
1. By lem-sc-finite-pushforward-locally-free, cover S by affine opens Spec A on
   which the finite algebra B of D is free of finite rank r. Finiteness makes q affine
   (def-weil-finite-integral-morphism). Since X is affine over S, write X_U = Spec C.
   Cover P over U by affine V = Spec R (lem-weil-pbc-affine-opens-basis).
   The affine fibre-product and free-base-change lemmas identify D_V with
   Spec(B tensor_A R), free over R with basis e_a tensor 1. The restricted F is an
   A-algebra map phi : C to B tensor_A R (lem-weil-pbc-morphism-into-affine-scheme).
2. The ideal of i is quasi-coherent and of finite type by
   def-sbc-closed-immersion-ideal-sheaf and
   lem-trf-closed-immersion-finite-presentation-ideal. By
   lem-weil-pbc-affine-finite-type-generating-sections choose finite generators h_beta
   on X_U. Put j_beta = phi(h_beta) and let J be the ideal they generate.
3. Expand j_beta in the finite R-basis. Let K_V be generated by its mr coefficients.
   Multiplication in B tensor_A R is R-linear, so the coefficients of any element of J
   lie in K_V. Coordinate functionals and their linear combinations show that K_V is
   exactly the ideal of all lambda(j), for j in J and R-linear lambda. Set
   L_V = Spec(R/K_V), a closed subscheme of V (def-weil-closed-immersion).
4. The finite free algebra is finite projective (def-weil-pbc-projective-module).
   By lem-pc-affine-containment-ideal, J vanishes over any R-algebra R' precisely when
   K_V vanishes there. The quotient universal property and
   lem-weil-pbc-morphism-into-affine-scheme identify this with the two factorizations.
   This uses no flatness of R'; the base-changed family sends c to phi(c) tensor 1.
5. Cover any test T over V by affine opens. By lem-weil-pbc-open-fibre-product these
   give a cover of D_T. Closed immersions are monomorphisms
   (lem-pc-proper-monomorphism-closed), so the local lifts agree on overlaps and glue
   uniquely by lem-weil-pbc-scheme-morphism-gluing. The criterion holds for all T.
6. On V intersect W, the pullbacks of L_V and L_W are closed
   (lem-weil-pbc-closed-immersion-base-change) and have the same test-scheme property.
   Testing on each gives inverse maps over the intersection. Monicity proves their
   composites are identities and gives the cocycle condition on triple overlaps.
7. Apply lem-weil-pbc-glue-schemes to these overlap isomorphisms and glue the maps
   into P by lem-weil-pbc-scheme-morphism-gluing. The resulting L restricts exactly
   to L_V over V; lem-weil-pbc-closed-immersion-affine-cover-criterion proves L to P
   is a closed immersion.
8. Its ideal restricts to the sheaf associated to K_V. The mr generators make it
   locally of finite type (lem-weil-pbc-quasi-coherent-finite-type-affine), so
   lem-trf-closed-immersion-finite-presentation-ideal proves finite presentation.
   Neither a finite cover of P nor a Noetherian hypothesis is needed.
9. For arbitrary t : T to P, cover T by the inverse images of the V. Step 5 gives
   the criterion locally; monicity and lem-weil-pbc-scheme-morphism-gluing give
   the equivalence and uniqueness globally, using lem-weil-pbc-open-fibre-product.
10. For any P' to P the base-changed coefficients are the images of the old ones.
    By lem-pc-closed-subscheme-base-change-ideal-image the given immersion
    L times_P P' to P' has exactly those ideals. They are finitely generated.
    The fibre-product universal property identifies its lifts with lifts to L;
    Step 9 then gives the condition for the induced family, with uniqueness.
    In rank zero K_V is zero and L_V = V, so empty sources are included. -/
theorem exists_finiteLocallyFreeSource_closedFactorizationLocus
    {S D X Z P : Scheme.{u}} (q : D ⟶ S) [q.IsFiniteLocallyFree]
    (x : X ⟶ S) [IsAffineHom x] [LocallyOfFinitePresentation x]
    (i : Z ⟶ X) [IsClosedImmersion i] [LocallyOfFinitePresentation i]
    (p : P ⟶ S) (F : pullback q p ⟶ X)
    (hF : F ≫ x = pullback.snd q p ≫ p) :
    ∃ (L : Scheme.{u}) (l : L ⟶ P),
      IsClosedImmersion l ∧ LocallyOfFinitePresentation l ∧
      (∀ (T : Scheme.{u}) (t : T ⟶ P),
        (∃! a : T ⟶ L, a ≫ l = t) ↔
          ∃! b : pullback q (t ≫ p) ⟶ Z,
            b ≫ i = pullback.map q (t ≫ p) q p (𝟙 D) t (𝟙 S)
              (by simp) (by simp) ≫ F) ∧
      ∀ (P' : Scheme.{u}) (g : P' ⟶ P),
        IsClosedImmersion (pullback.snd l g) ∧
        LocallyOfFinitePresentation (pullback.snd l g) ∧
        ∀ (T : Scheme.{u}) (t : T ⟶ P'),
          (∃! a : T ⟶ pullback l g, a ≫ pullback.snd l g = t) ↔
            ∃! b : pullback q ((t ≫ g) ≫ p) ⟶ Z,
              b ≫ i =
                pullback.map q ((t ≫ g) ≫ p) q (g ≫ p) (𝟙 D) t (𝟙 S)
                  (by simp) (by simp [Category.assoc]) ≫
                pullback.map q (g ≫ p) q p (𝟙 D) g (𝟙 S) (by simp) (by simp) ≫ F := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
